thread:
sam: quick check — running into a weird failure when using the node/ts SDK from a Next.js edge function (NEXT_RUNTIME=edge). Streaming responses start but then 403s after the first chunk. looks like the Authorization header isn't being sent on the stream continuation. repro below, anyone seen this?

sam: repro (simplified):
```js
export default async function handler(req) {
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood-inference.com/v1/streams', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'rw/small', prompt: 'hi' })
  })
  for await (const chunk of r.body) {
    // first chunk ok, then 403 on subsequent reads
    console.log(chunk.toString())
  }
}
```

noor: hmm. couple quick q's — are you using the sdk package (package name: `@redwood/inference`) or doing raw fetch? and which Node runtime/version is in the edge bundle?

sam: using `@redwood/inference@0.7.1` (edge entry). bundle target is Vercel Edge (V8 isolates). we build with next 13.5 + turbopack.

lee: i've hit similar but it was an auth-cookie vs header thing — edge envs sometimes strip `Authorization` unless you explicitly mark fetch as private or forward the header from middleware. also streaming in isolates can drop state if the runtime swaps. did you try making a direct fetch from the page/server vs calling the SDK helper?

sam: tried raw fetch (above) and sdk helper; same result. interesting note: when I run locally with `next dev` it works. only on Vercel edge deploy.

padma: two quick troubleshooting steps: 1) confirm the upstream (redwood) accepts the auth on the streaming handshake (first response). 2) enable verbose logs on the SDK to see if the client is re-opening the connection without headers. Also can you paste the network logs? :eyes:

sam: grabbing logs now. here's the fetch trace — initial 101/200-ish chunk, then a second request to the same endpoint with no Authorization header (403). looks like the fetch polyfill in the bundle is re-sending?

noor: could be Vite/Turbopack polyfill behaviour. in some cases bundlers replace `globalThis.fetch` with a lightweight shim that doesn't carry custom internal headers into streamed sub-requests. two potential quick fixes: set fetch to the runtime native in next.config.js (disable polyfill) or pin the SDK to the explicit edge build that uses the Web fetch API (we added an `edge` entrypoint in 0.7.2).

lee: example next.config to force native runtime fetch:
```js
/** next.config.js */
module.exports = {
  experimental: {
    runtime: {
      edge: true,
    },
    polyfills: { fetch: false } // pseudo-setting — check your bundler docs
  }
}
```

padma: another workaround is to pass the auth as a query param for streams (less ideal for security), or use a short-lived signed token from serverless function (server side) and call edge with that ephemeral token.

sam: ok i pushed a test with `@redwood/inference@0.7.2-edge` (edge entrypoint) and disabled our fetch polyfill — staging deploy shows continuous stream now, no mid-request 403s. good catch @noor.

noor: nice! can you open a quick PR to update the devex docs about the edge entrypoint and bundler caveats? add a short note for Next/Vercel about not polyfilling fetch and using the edge entry.

sam: will do. also filing a small SDK follow-up: add a runtime-detection warning when Authorization looks like it's being dropped (helpful debug message). filing issue now.

ci-bot: deploy succeeded for branch sam/edge-auth-fix — staging ✔️

lee: awesome. TL;DR to include in docs: use the SDK edge entrypoint (>=0.7.2-edge), avoid fetch polyfills in edge bundles, and if you must, surface auth via short-lived tokens.

padma: and add a snippet for verifying streams locally (how to simulate Vercel edge quirks) — that would reduce future flaps.

sam: agreement. PR incoming with docs + repro script. thanks all :party_parrot:
